WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.080
This is the T cell receptor.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.540
It tells a T cell that it has found an infected cell.

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:12.780
But inside the cell, its tail is only a few amino acids long.

4
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.280
It holds no enzyme anywhere, not even a kinase.

5
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:17.880
So how does it send a message?

6
00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:21.403
The alpha and beta chains do the recognising.

7
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:25.803
Beside them sit partner chains, called CD3 and zeta.

8
00:00:26.343 --> 00:00:27.803
Their tails reach into the cell.

9
00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:33.063
Each tail carries a short motif of two tyrosines, a fixed short distance apart.

10
00:00:33.763 --> 00:00:36.023
This motif is called an ITAM.

11
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:38.567
So where is the kinase?

12
00:00:39.007 --> 00:00:40.327
It is already in the membrane.

13
00:00:41.047 --> 00:00:47.367
A co-receptor, CD4 or CD8, grips the display protein MHC that the receptor reads.

14
00:00:48.007 --> 00:00:52.847
A share of the tyrosine kinase Lck rides on its inner tail, beside the partner

15
00:00:52.847 --> 00:00:56.807
tails. Lck then adds a phosphate to both tyrosines.

16
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.510
Now the reader arrives: ZAP-70, a second kinase.

17
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.870
It has two SH2 domains, side by side.

18
00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.110
Each domain grips one phosphotyrosine, and the pair is spaced to fit the two on one

19
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.250
tail. With one phosphate, it binds weakly.

20
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:18.650
It binds tightly only when both tyrosines carry a phosphate, so one stray phosphate

21
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.010
does not start a T cell.

22
00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:27.133
Docking lets Lck switch on ZAP-70, which phosphorylates LAT, a scaffold set in the

23
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:33.373
membrane. From LAT, one branch raises calcium, and NFAT enters the nucleus.

24
00:01:34.153 --> 00:01:38.363
A second runs through DAG and protein kinase C to NF-κB.

25
00:01:38.363 --> 00:01:42.093
A third loads Ras, and ERK helps build AP-1.

26
00:01:42.093 --> 00:01:45.733
The three meet on genes such as the one for interleukin-2.

27
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:51.653
Even this is not enough, because receptors vary almost at random, and some fit the

28
00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:52.773
body's own peptides.

29
00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:57.833
In classic experiments on cultured T cells, signal one alone did worse than nothing.

30
00:01:58.513 --> 00:02:01.793
The cells entered a lasting unresponsive state called anergy.

31
00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:08.553
For a fresh T cell, a second signal needs the receptor CD28 to bind a B7 protein on

32
00:02:08.553 --> 00:02:09.413
the presenting cell.

33
00:02:09.413 --> 00:02:13.713
Keep the rule for fresh T cells: two phosphates and two signals.
